use /lib
use /ui
use /ui/components/sidebar/Panel
from /scope/git import GitScope
from /scope/harness-integration/baked-prompts import BakedSystemPromptsScope
from /scope/project-data import ProjectDataScope
from /scope/project-directory import ProjectDirectoryScope
from /scope/prompt-mode/modes import PromptModesScope
from /ui/components/file-tree import FileTreeScope
from /ui/components/growing-text-input import GrowingTextInputScope
from /ui/components/scrollbar import ScrollbarScope
from /ui/components/sidebar import SidebarScope

export panel GitPanelScope:
    height: fit
    scrolls: true

    concept: {GitPanelConcept}

concept GitPanelConcept:
    pitch:
        A very basic git panel for the repository the
        ${ProjectDirectoryScope} is in, as the @{GitScope} knows it: see what
        has changed at a glance, write a commit message, commit, push, and
        pull.

    shape: {GitPanelShape}

shape GitPanelShape:
    description:
        A compact panel. It is shown only while the project is inside a git
        repository.

    look:
        The panel's top row, the branch, is its header: 32 pixels tall, on the
        ribbon's tab row colour, #333333 in dark mode. Beneath it, the panel's
        body is on the ribbon's command area colour, #222222 in dark mode,
        with 8 pixels of padding at either side and at the bottom, and 4
        pixels between one thing and the next. There is no line between the
        header and the body, nor between the file tree and the header: the
        change of colour is the edge. The header has, along its bottom, the 1
        pixel border in the body's colour, #222222 in dark mode, that every
        panel header has, as the @{SidebarScope} says. Every box and button in it is square,
        with no rounded corners and no border, and the Pull and Push buttons
        are square too.

    sizing:
        The panel is only as tall as its header and body need, along the
        bottom of its sidebar, up to half the sidebar's height; the file tree
        takes the rest. It is never taller than its contents need, however it
        is sized: as its contents grow, such as when a commit note is added,
        it grows with them up to its most, its body scrolling beyond that,
        and as they shrink, such as when the notes are cleared by a commit,
        it shrinks straight back down to fit them.

        The edge between the file tree and the panel's header can be dragged
        to resize it: dragged up, the panel may grow up to where the edge was
        dragged, but no taller than its contents need, and dragged down, it
        shrinks, as far as its header alone. The file tree always keeps at
        least 80 pixels. Once dragged, the panel's most is the height it was
        dragged to rather than half the sidebar, and it stays that height as
        the window is resized, giving way to the file tree's least height
        when the window is too short. The edge shows no line at rest, since
        the change of colour is the edge: while the pointer is over it, or it
        is being dragged, a line in the accent shows along it, and the cursor
        is a row-resize cursor.

    scrolling:
        When the panel is shorter than its contents, only the commit notes
        scroll. Its header, the branch with Pull and Push, stays at its top,
        and the summary stays beneath it; the message box, Generate, and
        Commit stay fixed along its bottom, always in view, however many notes
        there are. The notes, between the summary and the message box, fill
        what is left and scroll within it, with a @{ScrollbarScope} along
        their right only while they overflow. Everything else is always as
        tall as it needs, so shrinking the panel takes room from the notes
        first, down to nothing; only once the notes have no room left does the
        rest give way, the summary last before the header. The body's colour
        runs all the way down the panel.

    branch:
        The top row shows a branch icon, then the branch from the
        @{GitScope}, or "Detached HEAD", in regular text, beside how many
        commits it is ahead of its upstream (↑) and behind it (↓), muted, each
        only when not zero. At the right end are a Pull button, a
        cloud-download icon, and a Push button, a cloud-upload icon, ghost
        icon buttons with tooltips.

    summary:
        Below the branch, a line counts the @{GitScope}'s changes, leaving out
        any kind with none, as "2 modified · 1 untracked", or "No changes".
        At the right end of that line, how many lines were added (+, in
        green) and removed (−, in red). The count is in small, muted text. The files themselves are not listed, since the @{FileTreeScope}
        shows them. The summary refreshes as the @{GitScope} does, and
        straight after anything the panel does.

    commitNotes:
        Above the message box, a box for each commit note, in the order they
        were added, on the ribbon's tab row colour, holding the note in small
        text that wraps onto as many lines as it needs, growing to fit it, and
        can be edited; <Enter> in a note starts a new line within it. A
        small button to remove it shows at its top right while it is hovered. Each task that adds a note, as the
        @{PromptModesScope} says, adds a one-line summary of what it changed,
        once the harness has written it, quickly, in the same way as a written
        message: from what the task asked and the harness's own summary of what
        it did, at most 72 characters in the imperative mood, as
        commit-note.pi, of the @{BakedSystemPromptsScope}, asks. Notes are
        saved with the project's data, as the @{ProjectDataScope} says, as they
        are added, edited, and removed, so they survive a restart, and are never
        themselves committed.

    message:
        A commit message box, a @{GrowingTextInputScope} growing to 10 rows at
        most, so it starts at a single row. On the tree's darkest surface, #111111 in dark mode,
        in the editor's monospace font, it reads "Commit Message…" while
        empty.

    writingAMessage:
        A "Generate" button, with a sparkles icon, on the ribbon's tab row
        colour, at the left of the row beneath the message box, has the harness write
        the commit message from what has changed, and puts it in the message
        box, replacing what was there. It is quick: a small, fast model, at
        low effort, with no tools, handed only the list of changed files and
        as much of the diff as fits in a small, fixed amount. The message is a
        summary line of at most 72 characters in the imperative mood,
        followed, only if it helps, by a few short bullet points, as
        commit-message.pi, of the @{BakedSystemPromptsScope}, asks. The button
        is disabled while there are no changes.

    committing:
        A primary "Commit" button, at the right end of that row, on the
        ribbon's button colour, #444444 in dark mode, with no icon, commits every change, new files included.
        The commit message is what was typed, then a blank line, then each
        note that isn't blank as a line of its own starting "- "; with nothing
        typed, it is the notes alone. Once committed, the message box and the
        notes are cleared. It is disabled while there is neither a message nor
        a note, or there are no changes.

    pushingAndPulling:
        Push pushes the branch, setting its upstream on origin first if it has
        none.

        Pull fetches the branch's upstream. With nothing new it says the
        branch is already up to date. When only the upstream has moved on, the
        branch fast-forwards to it. When both have moved on, the upstream is
        merged in automatically, as long as the merge has no conflicts. If it
        would conflict, the pull is cancelled before anything changes: the
        branch, its files, and the index stay exactly as they were, no merge is
        left in progress, and an error names the files that would have
        conflicted. Pull is disabled while the branch has no upstream.

    feedback:
        While the panel is committing, pushing, pulling, or writing a message,
        that button shows a spinner and the others are disabled. Committing,
        pushing, and pulling say so in a notification when they succeed; a pull
        says whether it was already up to date, pulled, or pulled and merged.
        Anything that fails says why in an error notification.
